/// \defgroup grp_w1023b_wwmath_line_tri_frustum WWMath: frustum, line segment and triangle queries
/// \ingroup grp_w3d_rendering
/// \brief A camera's view frustum, the tests that classify points and boxes
///        against it, and the line-segment and triangle helpers behind ray
///        casts, picking and road geometry.
///
/// Four small units of Westwood's WWMath library, the geometry layer under
/// the W3D renderer (\ref sub_w3d_rendering). All four have Zero Hour
/// counterparts, and the algorithms are Zero Hour's apart from the differences
/// listed below.
///
/// - FrustumClass and FrustumClass::Init (frustum_init.cpp): the six
///   bounding planes, eight corners and axis-aligned bound of a camera's view
///   volume, built from a camera transform, a view window and two clip
///   distances.
/// - The CollisionMath::Overlap_Test overloads that take a FrustumClass
///   (colmathfrustum.cpp): classify a point, box, oriented box or triangle
///   against the six planes.
/// - LineSegClass (lineseg.cpp): a finite segment, used as the ray in ray
///   casts and picking and as an edge in road geometry, with transform,
///   random-segment, closest-point and line-line operations.
/// - TriClass (tri.cpp): the triangle view the collision code works with, its
///   point-containment test and the dominant-plane selection behind it.
///
/// \par Conventions
/// - A PlaneClass is a unit normal N and a distance D. The signed distance of
///   a point from the plane is N dot point minus D, so a positive value means
///   the point is in front of the plane.
/// - FrustumClass builds its planes so that the normals point out of the
///   view volume. In front of a frustum plane therefore means outside the
///   frustum, and behind every plane means inside.
/// - CollisionMath::OverlapType is the answer of the whole Overlap_Test
///   family, which classifies the second operand against the first. The
///   enumerators are POS (1), NEG (2), ON (4) and BOTH (8), with OUTSIDE
///   meaning POS, INSIDE meaning NEG and OVERLAPPED meaning BOTH. The
///   frustum overloads return only OUTSIDE, INSIDE or OVERLAPPED.
/// - The frustum tests are conservative. OUTSIDE is returned only when one
///   plane has the whole volume in front of it, and INSIDE only when every
///   plane has the whole volume behind it. Anything else, including a volume
///   that is outside the frustum but not wholly in front of any single plane
///   (near a frustum edge or corner), comes back as OVERLAPPED. The source
///   carries a note that the tests do not catch every case.
/// - A fraction, wherever a function here returns or accepts one, is a
///   position along a segment: 0 is the start point and 1 the end point.
///
/// \par Differences from Zero Hour
/// - FrustumClass::Init takes a sixth parameter, a flag that selects an
///   orthographic frustum. Zero Hour's version takes five arguments and always
///   builds a perspective frustum. BFME 2 also has two small five-argument
///   entry points that forward to Init with the flag fixed to false or true;
///   they were unnamed when this was written and are not documented here.
/// - LineSegClass::Find_Intersection has a caller in the GameLogic AI code,
///   the pathfinder's rounding of path corners. No Zero Hour counterpart was
///   found: Zero Hour's GameEngine sources do not use LineSegClass at all, and
///   the only Zero Hour users of Find_Intersection found are the road,
///   line-renderer and LineSegClass sources. This makes the function part of
///   the simulation in BFME 2; see the Modding notes below.
/// - Zero Hour's tri.cpp also carries older point-containment variants that are
///   compiled out. They are not part of the live algorithm, and BFME 2 follows
///   the live one.
///
/// \par Plane tests inside the frustum overloads
/// In Zero Hour's source the plane-against-point and plane-against-AABoxClass
/// tests are inline functions in colmathplane.h, which colmathinlines.h pulls
/// in, so they expand inside the point and AABoxClass frustum overloads; the
/// plane tests for triangles, spheres and oriented boxes are out-of-line
/// functions that the other overloads call. Retail's frustum overloads have the
/// same split: the point and AABoxClass bodies carry their plane tests inline,
/// and the triangle, sphere and oriented-box bodies call the out-of-line plane
/// tests. The arithmetic is the same. This rests on the byte-matched bodies and
/// the size of the sibling bodies, not on a separate disassembly.
///
/// \par Where BFME 2 uses it
/// The callers below were read from the project's caller tool
/// (tools/callers_of.py) when this was written. It reports callers among named
/// functions only, so the lists are not exhaustive and can grow as more of the
/// engine is named; rerun the tool for the current list. Functions here with
/// no caller listed (the point, triangle and oriented-box frustum tests,
/// LineSegClass::Set_Random and TriClass::Find_Dominant_Plane) had none named
/// at that time.
/// - FrustumClass::Init: CameraClass::Update_Frustum, which builds the
///   camera's world-space frustum and its view-space frustum, and the two
///   five-argument forwarding entry points described above (unnamed when this
///   was written).
/// - Frustum against AABoxClass: GridCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects through
///   GridCullSystemClass::collect_objects_in_leaf, W3DVolumetricShadow's
///   updateMeshVolume and updateVolumes, and a W3DProjectedShadowManager
///   method.
/// - LineSegClass: W3DView::pickDrawable, W3DView::lookAt and
///   W3DView::screenToTerrain and BaseHeightMapRenderObjClass::Cast_Ray call
///   its two-point Set; MeshClass::Cast_Ray moves the ray into mesh space with
///   the transforming Set; W3DRoadBuffer, SegmentedLineClass::Cast_Ray and
///   StreakLineClass::Cast_Ray call the line queries. In game logic, the
///   pathfinder's path-corner rounding in the GameLogic AI code builds
///   segments and calls Find_Intersection; it is the logic-side caller found
///   when this was written. The current caller list of any function comes
///   from tools/callers_of.py.
/// - TriClass: CollisionMath::Collide for a line segment against a triangle,
///   which BaseHeightMapRenderObjClass::Cast_Ray,
///   W3DTerrainBackground::castTriangle,
///   MeshGeometryClass::cast_ray_brute_force and
///   AABTreeClass::Cast_Ray_To_Polys call.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The code in this group has no tunables of its own. Its inputs are geometry
/// that other code supplies: a camera's view window, clip distances and
/// transform for the frustum, object bounds for the overlap tests, and mouse
/// or camera rays for the segment queries. What is fixed in code: a frustum
/// always has six planes and eight corners (the planes_passed bitmask has one
/// bit per plane), and the overlap tests treat a distance within one millionth
/// of a world unit as lying on a plane.
///
/// Which mode FrustumClass::Init builds follows from the camera's projection
/// type, because CameraClass::Update_Frustum passes the orthographic flag for
/// any camera whose projection type is not perspective. The one data setting
/// traced to that choice is the OrthoCamera field of the Mouse INI block: when
/// it is true, the 3D mouse-cursor camera that W3DMouse::initW3DAssets creates
/// is set to orthographic projection, and the Mouse OrthoZoom field then
/// multiplies the scale of the cursor models loaded there. The cursor
/// camera's view window is a fixed (-1,-1) to (1,1) window. W3DMouse::draw
/// renders the 3D interface scene with that camera, and applying a camera
/// updates its frustum, so the orthographic mode follows. Other cameras can
/// also be orthographic; this page does not enumerate them.
///
/// The shape of generated road geometry depends on these functions:
/// W3DRoadBuffer joins and mitres road segments with
/// LineSegClass::Find_Intersection and LineSegClass::Find_Point_Closest_To.
///
/// \warning Most callers named above are render-side code: camera, culling,
///          shadows, picking and road drawing. LineSegClass::Find_Intersection
///          is not only render-side. BFME 2's pathfinder path-corner rounding
///          in the GameLogic AI code (the AI path-smoothing code) also calls
///          it, with no Zero Hour counterpart found, and appends path nodes
///          computed from its result. Changing that function, or the
///          LineSegClass construction and accessors that code relies on, can
///          therefore change path nodes and break lockstep and replay
///          compatibility. tools/callers_of.py gives the current caller list.
///
/// LineSegClass::Set_Random is the other function to watch: it draws from the
/// C runtime's rand sequence, which client-side code also draws from and
/// reseeds, and which is not the deterministic logic random generator. A game
/// logic caller's values could therefore differ between peers and break
/// lockstep and replay compatibility. No caller was named when this was
/// written. This page does not establish that
/// no other logic-side code reaches the rest of the group, since the caller
/// tool lists named callers only.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The code is single-precision float arithmetic with no allocation and no
///   Direct3D or Win32 dependency, apart from the C runtime rand inside
///   LineSegClass::Set_Random, so it ports without a platform layer.
/// - The box overlap tests read the sign of a float through an integer
///   pointer cast (WWMath::Fast_Is_Float_Positive). A compiler that enforces
///   strict aliasing needs a memcpy or bit-cast there.
/// - The coincidence tolerance is an absolute one millionth. At world
///   coordinates in the thousands a float cannot resolve differences that
///   small, so a classification as lying on a plane in practice means the
///   computed distance is almost exactly zero.
/// - FrustumClass::Init builds the corners from a finite far distance, so a
///   renderer with an infinite far plane would still need a finite distance
///   for culling. Its perspective mode also mirrors a view window that is not
///   centred on the viewing axis (see the notes on FrustumClass::Init), so a
///   renderer that uses off-centre projections must check that first.
/// - The frustum tests, picking and road code run on the render/client path;
///   LineSegClass::Find_Intersection is also reached from game logic (see the
///   Modding notes). A port that evaluates its float arithmetic differently
///   from retail (another instruction set, precision mode or fused
///   multiply-add) could therefore change path nodes, which matters for lockstep
///   between peers and for replays.
///
/// \see \ref grp_w1_wwmath_matrix4_aabox for AABoxClass and the other box
///      types these tests take


/// \file WWMath/colmathfrustum.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_wwmath_line_tri_frustum
/// \brief The CollisionMath::Overlap_Test overloads whose first operand is a
///        FrustumClass.
///
/// The algorithms are Zero Hour's colmathfrustum.cpp: the point, triangle,
/// sphere, AABoxClass and OBBoxClass overloads, and the OBBoxClass overload
/// that also takes a planes_passed mask. Zero Hour defines the AABoxClass
/// overload with a planes_passed mask as an inline function in
/// colmathfrustum.h, and the BFME 1 header has the same inline, so it is not
/// one of the overloads documented here. The sphere overload has the same
/// shape as the triangle one and is not described separately.
///
/// The point and AABoxClass overloads carry their plane tests inline. Zero
/// Hour gets the same expressions from the inline functions in colmathplane.h,
/// which colmathinlines.h pulls in; see the group page.


/// \file WWMath/frustum_init.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_wwmath_line_tri_frustum
/// \brief FrustumClass::Init, which builds a frustum from a camera.
///
/// The BFME 2 form of Zero Hour's FrustumClass::Init, with the orthographic
/// parameter added. The rest of FrustumClass is in the header, which holds the
/// data members and the Get_Bound_Min and Get_Bound_Max accessors. Neither the
/// BFME 1 header nor Zero Hour's declares the sixth parameter.


/// \file WWMath/lineseg.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_wwmath_line_tri_frustum
/// \brief The out-of-line members of LineSegClass.
///
/// In Zero Hour these are the members of LineSegClass defined out of line: the
/// transforming Set, Set_Random, Find_Point_Closest_To and Find_Intersection.
/// The constructors, the two-point Set, Compute_Point, the getters and the
/// protected recalculate are inline in lineseg.h. The algorithms here are Zero
/// Hour's.


/// \file WWMath/tri.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_wwmath_line_tri_frustum
/// \brief The out-of-line members of TriClass and the dominant-plane helper
///        they share.
///
/// In Zero Hour these are TriClass::Contains_Point, TriClass::Find_Dominant_Plane
/// and the file-local find_dominant_plane, whose algorithms BFME 2 shares.
/// TriClass::Compute_Normal and the free helpers Point_In_Triangle_2D and
/// Cast_Semi_Infinite_Axis_Aligned_Ray_To_Triangle are inline in tri.h.


/// \class CollisionMath
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_wwmath_line_tri_frustum
/// \brief The static toolbox of low-level geometric queries: overlap
///        classification, boolean intersection and swept collision between
///        planes, spheres, boxes, triangles, line segments and frustums.
///
/// CollisionMath has no instances and no per-object state. It is declared in
/// colmath.h and implemented across many units, most of them named
/// colmath*.cpp, roughly one per pair of shapes. The declarations fall into
/// three families.
/// - Intersection_Test overloads return a bool for box against triangle,
///   box against box and similar pairs.
/// - Overlap_Test overloads classify the second operand against the first and
///   return an OverlapType. The first operand is the volume: an axis-aligned
///   or general plane, a sphere, an AABoxClass, an OBBoxClass or a
///   FrustumClass; one more form takes an axis-aligned range as a minimum and
///   maximum vector and a line segment. This group documents the
///   FrustumClass overloads.
/// - Collide overloads cast a line segment or a moving box into a plane,
///   triangle, sphere or box and fill a CastResultStruct. That structure
///   starts with Fraction 1 and a zero normal. The Collide of a line segment
///   against a triangle, for one, only records a hit nearer than the Fraction
///   already stored, so one structure can be passed through many calls to
///   keep the nearest hit.
///
/// The shared data is a private static coincidence tolerance, one millionth,
/// and a static statistics block; both are defined in colmath.cpp (see
/// \ref grp_w1_wwmath_matrix4_aabox). Private helpers combine per-vertex
/// results into one OverlapType: if every vertex is ON the result is ON, if
/// every vertex is POS or ON it is POS, if every vertex is NEG or ON it is
/// NEG, and otherwise BOTH. The plane tests for lines and triangles use them.
///
/// \par Frustum overloads
/// Each frustum overload walks the six planes of the FrustumClass and asks the
/// plane-against-shape test for a verdict. It returns OUTSIDE at the first
/// plane that puts the shape wholly in front, ORs the verdicts together
/// otherwise, and answers INSIDE only if the OR equals NEG. An ON verdict
/// (point or triangle touching a plane) therefore makes the answer
/// OVERLAPPED. See
/// CollisionMath::Overlap_Test(const FrustumClass &, const AABoxClass &) for
/// the usual caller pattern.
///
/// \par Where BFME 2 uses it
/// Culling code asks the frustum overloads before drawing or building shadow
/// volumes; ray casts and mouse picking use the Collide overloads, for example
/// CollisionMath::Collide for a line segment against a triangle, which terrain
/// and mesh ray casts call. The callers named in this documentation are
/// culling, shadow, picking and ray-cast code.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The coincidence tolerance is a constant in code and has no setting. What
/// the frustum overloads classify depends on the frustum the caller built; see
/// the group page for how a camera's projection type reaches it.
///
/// \see FrustumClass, LineSegClass, TriClass


/// \fn static CollisionMath::OverlapType CollisionMath::Overlap_Test(const FrustumClass &frustum, const AABoxClass &box)
/// \brief Classifies an axis-aligned box against a frustum: wholly outside,
///        wholly inside, or overlapping (conservative).
///
/// This is the visibility test for cull boxes. GridCullSystemClass keeps every
/// object whose cull box does not come back OUTSIDE
/// (GridCullSystemClass::collect_objects_in_leaf, reached from
/// GridCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects), so INSIDE and OVERLAPPED are treated
/// alike there. The shadow code uses a two-stage pattern: W3DVolumetricShadow
/// tests a volume's bounding sphere against the shadow camera's frustum first
/// and calls this overload for a more accurate answer only when the sphere
/// test says OVERLAPPED. Zero Hour's volumetric shadow code does the same.
/// CameraClass::Cull_Box is a separate reject-only test and does not call this.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// For each of the six planes the test picks the box corner that lies furthest
/// along the plane normal (per axis, plus the half-size if the normal
/// component's sign bit is clear, otherwise minus it) and the opposite corner,
/// and measures the signed distance of both. If even the rearmost corner is
/// more than the tolerance in front of the plane, the whole box is in front
/// and the answer is OUTSIDE at once. If even the foremost corner is more than
/// the tolerance behind the plane, the box is wholly behind that plane.
/// Otherwise it straddles the plane. The per-plane results are ORed, and the
/// box is INSIDE only if every plane put it wholly behind. That is two corner
/// tests per plane, not eight.
///
/// A negative zero normal component counts as negative, because the test reads
/// the sign bit.
///
/// The source notes that the test does not catch every case: a box can be
/// outside the frustum without being wholly in front of any single plane, and
/// is then reported OVERLAPPED.
///
/// \param frustum The view volume. Its planes must have outward normals, as
///                FrustumClass::Init makes them.
/// \param box     The box to classify, as centre and half-size, in the same
///                space as the frustum.
/// \return OUTSIDE, INSIDE or OVERLAPPED.
///
/// \note In retail this overload carries the plane-against-box test and its
///       point test inline, unlike the triangle, sphere and oriented-box
///       overloads, which call out-of-line plane tests. Zero Hour gets the same
///       code from the inline functions in colmathplane.h, which
///       colmathinlines.h pulls in. This rests on the byte-matched body and the
///       size of the sibling bodies, not on a separate disassembly.
/// \see CollisionMath::Overlap_Test(const FrustumClass &, const Vector3 &),
///      FrustumClass::Init, GridCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects


/// \fn static CollisionMath::OverlapType CollisionMath::Overlap_Test(const FrustumClass &frustum, const Vector3 &point)
/// \brief Classifies a point against a frustum: outside, inside, or on the
///        boundary (reported as overlapped).
///
/// The point version of the frustum test. A point that is exactly on one of
/// the six planes, or within the tolerance of it, produces an ON verdict from
/// that plane, and any ON verdict makes the result OVERLAPPED, so INSIDE
/// means strictly inside every plane by more than the tolerance. Zero Hour:
/// the terrain visibility code in HeightMap.cpp (the static check function)
/// uses it on height-field grid points and accepts only INSIDE when it
/// widens the range of visible tiles. The caller tool named no BFME 2 caller
/// of this overload when this was written, so whether retail uses it is not
/// established here.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// For each plane the signed distance is N dot point minus D. A distance
/// above the tolerance is POS, below minus the tolerance is NEG, anything
/// between is ON. The first POS returns OUTSIDE; the verdicts are ORed and the
/// result is INSIDE only if the OR equals NEG.
///
/// \param frustum The view volume, with outward plane normals.
/// \param point   The position to classify, in the frustum's space.
/// \return OUTSIDE if the point is in front of any plane, INSIDE if it is
///         behind all six, otherwise OVERLAPPED.
///
/// \note As with the box overload, retail carries the point test for each plane
///       inline in this overload. Zero Hour gets it from the inline function in
///       colmathplane.h, which colmathinlines.h pulls in. This rests on the
///       byte-matched body and the size of the sibling bodies, not on a
///       separate disassembly.
/// \see CollisionMath::Overlap_Test(const FrustumClass &, const AABoxClass &)


/// \fn static CollisionMath::OverlapType CollisionMath::Overlap_Test(const FrustumClass &frustum, const OBBoxClass &box, int &planes_passed)
/// \brief Classifies an oriented box against a frustum while remembering which
///        planes the box is wholly inside, so that tests for contained
///        volumes can skip those planes.
///
/// The hierarchical form of the frustum test, for culling trees. The caller
/// passes zero at the root. The function sets the bit for plane i in
/// planes_passed whenever the box is wholly behind that plane, and on the way
/// down the tree the caller hands the updated mask to the tests of each
/// child. A plane whose bit is already set is not examined again and counts
/// as INSIDE, because a child lies within its parent. The contract is
/// therefore valid only for volumes contained in the volume that set the bits.
/// Zero Hour: AABTreeCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects_Recursive does exactly
/// this with the AABoxClass version of the test (an inline in
/// colmathfrustum.h). The caller tool named no caller of the oriented-box
/// version when this was written.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Plane i is bit 1 shifted left by i, so six bits are used. A plane that is
/// not yet known to pass is tested with the plane-against-OBBoxClass overload:
/// OUTSIDE returns at once, INSIDE sets the plane's bit, and any other result
/// leaves it clear. The verdicts are ORed, with INSIDE counted for every
/// skipped plane, and the result is INSIDE only if the OR equals NEG.
///
/// \param frustum      The view volume, with outward plane normals.
/// \param box          The oriented box to classify.
/// \param planes_passed [in,out] Bitmask of planes already known to contain
///                     the volume; updated as planes are found to contain the
///                     box. Pass zero when starting a new query. After an
///                     OUTSIDE result it holds only the bits found before the
///                     rejecting plane.
/// \return OUTSIDE, INSIDE or OVERLAPPED.
///
/// \warning Passing a mask that was set for a different volume can skip
///          planes the box actually crosses and give a wrong INSIDE.
/// \see CollisionMath::Overlap_Test(const FrustumClass &, const OBBoxClass &)


/// \fn static CollisionMath::OverlapType CollisionMath::Overlap_Test(const FrustumClass &frustum, const OBBoxClass &box)
/// \brief Classifies an oriented box against a frustum: wholly outside,
///        wholly inside, or overlapping (conservative).
///
/// The same loop as the AABoxClass overload, with the per-plane verdict coming
/// from the plane-against-OBBoxClass test, which rotates the plane normal into
/// the box's own axes to find the far corner. It had no named caller when this
/// was written.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Each of the six planes is tested with CollisionMath::Overlap_Test for a
/// plane against an oriented box. OUTSIDE returns immediately, the verdicts
/// are ORed, and the result is INSIDE only if the OR equals NEG.
///
/// \param frustum The view volume, with outward plane normals.
/// \param box     The oriented box to classify.
/// \return OUTSIDE, INSIDE or OVERLAPPED.
/// \see CollisionMath::Overlap_Test(const FrustumClass &, const OBBoxClass &, int &)


/// \fn static CollisionMath::OverlapType CollisionMath::Overlap_Test(const FrustumClass &frustum, const TriClass &tri)
/// \brief Classifies a triangle against a frustum: wholly outside, wholly
///        inside, or overlapping (conservative).
///
/// The same loop again, with the verdict from the plane-against-triangle test,
/// which looks only at the triangle's three vertices. OUTSIDE is exact: if all
/// three vertices are in front of one plane, so is the whole triangle. A
/// triangle that lies outside the frustum but is not wholly in front of any
/// one plane, for example one cutting across a frustum corner, is reported
/// OVERLAPPED. It had no named caller when this was written.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Each plane test classifies the three vertices (POS, NEG or ON, with the
/// tolerance) and combines them: all POS or ON gives POS, all NEG or ON gives
/// NEG, all ON gives ON, anything else BOTH. The frustum then returns OUTSIDE
/// for a POS, ORs the verdicts and answers INSIDE only if the OR equals NEG.
/// A triangle lying in a frustum plane yields ON and so OVERLAPPED.
///
/// \param frustum The view volume, with outward plane normals.
/// \param tri     The triangle to classify. Only its vertices are read.
/// \return OUTSIDE, INSIDE or OVERLAPPED.
/// \see TriClass


/// \class FrustumClass
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_wwmath_line_tri_frustum
/// \brief The view volume of a camera: six outward-facing planes, eight
///        corners and an axis-aligned bound, filled in by FrustumClass::Init.
///
/// FrustumClass is a plain value type with public data and no virtual
/// functions. It stores the camera transform it was built with, six
/// PlaneClass planes in a fixed order (near, four side planes, far), eight
/// corner points, and the minimum and maximum of the corners as an
/// axis-aligned bound (Get_Bound_Min and Get_Bound_Max).
///
/// The corners are numbered 0 to 3 on the near plane and 4 to 7 on the far
/// plane, in the same order, so corner i and corner i plus 4 are the two ends
/// of one edge of the volume. BaseHeightMapRenderObjClass::getMaximumVisibleBox
/// relies on this: it clips each such edge against a ground plane to find the
/// part of the terrain the camera can see. The planes are numbered by corner
/// triple: plane 0 is built from corners 0, 3, 1 and plane 5 from corners
/// 4, 7, 6, with the four side planes between.
///
/// The source labels the side planes bottom, right, top and left, and the
/// corners upper-left, upper-right, lower-left and lower-right, as seen from
/// the camera with camera-space x to the right and y up. The two sets of labels
/// disagree, and which one holds depends on the mode. For a camera matrix that
/// is not reflected, perspective mode scales the window by a negative
/// distance, which negates x and y, so corner 0 ends up at the lower right: the
/// plane labels hold and the corner comment does not. In orthographic mode
/// nothing is negated, so the corner comment holds and the plane labels do
/// not (planes 1 to 4 are then the top, left, bottom and right). Treat both as
/// unreliable and use the PlaneClass::Set corner triples instead.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// CameraClass holds two FrustumClass values by value, a world-space one and a
/// view-space one built with an identity transform, and rebuilds them lazily
/// in CameraClass::Update_Frustum when the camera has changed. Code that needs
/// the volume asks CameraClass::Get_Frustum, which updates it first. The class
/// is copied by value; the volumetric shadow code, for one, copies the
/// camera's frustum into a variable that its culling tests then read.
///
/// \par Invariants
/// Only FrustumClass::Init keeps the planes, corners and bound consistent with
/// each other and with the stored transform. The data members are public,
/// but writing one directly leaves the others stale.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The class has no tunables: the planes, corners and tolerance are fixed in
/// code. A camera's view window and clip distances reach the frustum through
/// CameraClass::Update_Frustum, and the camera's projection type chooses the
/// mode of FrustumClass::Init. The 3D mouse-cursor camera becomes orthographic
/// when the Mouse INI OrthoCamera field is true, with a fixed (-1,-1) to (1,1)
/// view window; the group page has the details.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// See the group page. A frustum always has six planes and eight corners, and
/// the planes_passed mask of the hierarchical tests has one bit per plane.
///
/// \see FrustumClass::Init, CollisionMath, CameraClass::Update_Frustum


/// \fn void FrustumClass::Init(const Matrix3D &camera, const Vector2 &viewport_min, const Vector2 &viewport_max, float znear, float zfar, bool ortho)
/// \brief Builds the frustum of a camera: corners, six outward-facing planes
///        and the bound, in the space of the supplied camera transform.
///
/// CameraClass::Update_Frustum calls it. It reads the
/// camera's view window and clip distances, negates the distances so that they
/// point down the camera's negative Z axis, and calls Init twice: once with the
/// camera's world transform, for the world-space frustum that culling uses, and
/// once with an identity matrix, for the view-space frustum. It passes false
/// for a perspective camera and true for any other projection type. BFME 2
/// also has two small five-argument entry points that call this function with
/// the flag fixed to false or true; they were unnamed when this was written.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The camera transform is stored in the frustum.
/// - If both znear and zfar are positive they are negated, because the camera
///   looks down negative Z. If only one is positive, nothing is flipped.
/// - The code looks at whether the camera matrix is mirrored: if the cross
///   product of its X and Y axes points opposite to its Z axis, the left and
///   right corner assignments are swapped. This keeps the corner order, and so
///   the winding of the plane constructions below, consistent after the
///   transform; the source does not state its reason, so that explanation is
///   inferred from the plane construction.
/// - Each corner starts as (view-window x, view-window y, 1). A far corner
///   starts as a copy of the near corner with the same index.
/// - Perspective (ortho false): near corners are scaled by znear and far
///   corners by zfar, so the magnitudes of x and y grow with distance, and z
///   becomes znear or zfar. The view window is therefore the window at unit
///   distance. Because znear and zfar are normally negative, the scaling also
///   negates x and y. For a window centred on the viewing axis, which is what
///   CameraClass::Set_View_Plane makes from a field of view, the volume is the
///   same either way; a window that is not centred would come out mirrored
///   through the viewing axis. This follows from the arithmetic; whether any
///   camera in the game uses an off-centre window is not established here.
/// - Orthographic (ortho true): only z is set, to znear or zfar. x and y are
///   used as given, without the negation, so the view window is the window
///   itself in camera units and the volume is a box.
/// - All eight corners are transformed by the camera transform with
///   Matrix3D::Transform_Vector, which takes them out of camera space.
/// - Each plane is made by PlaneClass::Set from three corners, in the order
///   near (corners 0, 3, 1), then the four side planes, then far (corners 4,
///   7, 6). PlaneClass::Set takes the normal as the normalised cross product
///   of the two edges from its first point and sets D to the dot of the normal
///   and the first point. If three corners are collinear (for example when the
///   view window has no width or height) Set returns the default plane with
///   normal (0, 0, 1) and distance 0. The source's side-plane and corner
///   labels hold in only one mode each; see FrustumClass.
/// - BoundMin and BoundMax are the componentwise minimum and maximum of the
///   eight transformed corners.
///
/// \param camera       Transform of the camera. Its negative Z axis is the
///                     viewing direction. Pass an identity matrix for a
///                     view-space frustum.
/// \param viewport_min Minimum corner of the view window. In perspective
///                     mode it is the window at unit distance in front of the
///                     camera, so for a centred window made by
///                     CameraClass::Set_View_Plane it holds the negative
///                     tangents of the half field-of-view angles; in
///                     orthographic mode it is the window itself.
/// \param viewport_max Maximum corner of the view window, in the same terms.
/// \param znear        Camera-space Z of the near clip plane; negative in
///                     front of the camera. A positive value is negated when
///                     zfar is also positive.
/// \param zfar         Camera-space Z of the far clip plane, with the same
///                     sign rule. It must be finite.
/// \param ortho        false builds a perspective frustum, true an
///                     orthographic one.
///
/// \note Nothing checks that znear is nearer than zfar or that the window has
///       any area.
/// \note Zero Hour's FrustumClass::Init has no ortho parameter and always
///       scales the corners with depth; its camera has an orthographic
///       projection type, but the frustum it builds for one is perspective.
///       The BFME 2 sixth parameter and its meaning come from the retail body
///       and from CameraClass::Update_Frustum, which passes it. The name ortho
///       is inferred from that behaviour, not recovered from retail.
/// \see CameraClass::Update_Frustum, PlaneClass::Set


/// \class LineSegClass
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_wwmath_line_tri_frustum
/// \brief A finite line segment with its direction and length cached: the ray
///        of W3D's ray casts and a helper for road geometry.
///
/// LineSegClass is a value type of two points, P0 and P1, plus three derived
/// values: DP (P1 minus P0), Dir (DP normalised) and Length (the length of
/// DP). The data members are protected, so the derived values stay consistent:
/// the two-point constructor and the two-point Set call a protected
/// recalculate, and getters (Get_P0, Get_P1, Get_DP, Get_Dir, Get_Length)
/// expose them. A
/// segment of zero length has a zero Dir and Length 0. Compute_Point turns a
/// fraction t into the point P0 plus t times DP.
///
/// \par How BFME 2 uses it
/// - Picking. W3DView::pickDrawable, W3DView::screenToTerrain and
///   W3DView::lookAt build segments with the two-point Set, and the render
///   objects' Cast_Ray methods take the segment as the ray of a
///   RayCollisionTestClass. BaseHeightMapRenderObjClass::Cast_Ray calls the
///   two-point Set as well.
/// - Ray into object space. MeshClass::Cast_Ray transforms the world-space ray
///   into mesh space with the transforming Set, through the transforming
///   copy constructor of RayCollisionTestClass.
/// - Roads. W3DRoadBuffer (insertCrossTypeJoins, miter, insertCurveSegmentAt,
///   findCrossTypeJoinVector) uses Find_Intersection and Find_Point_Closest_To.
/// - Line renderers. SegmentedLineClass::Cast_Ray and StreakLineClass::Cast_Ray
///   test the ray against each segment of the line with Find_Intersection.
/// - Pathfinding. The pathfinder's path-corner rounding in the GameLogic AI
///   code (the AI path-smoothing code) builds LineSegClass legs with the
///   two-point constructor, reads their lengths and directions, offsets them
///   sideways and calls Find_Intersection for the centre of the corner arc,
///   then appends path nodes around that centre. No Zero Hour counterpart was
///   found: Zero Hour's GameEngine sources do not use LineSegClass. The other
///   uses listed above are client-side render and drawing code.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Generated road geometry depends on Find_Intersection and
/// Find_Point_Closest_To (see the group page).
///
/// \warning LineSegClass is also used by game logic. The AI path-smoothing code
///          relies on Find_Intersection and on the segment construction and
///          accessors, and the path nodes it appends feed the lockstep
///          simulation. A change to this class's arithmetic can change path
///          nodes and break lockstep and replay compatibility. Set_Random is
///          the other function to watch; see the group page.
///
/// \see LineSegClass::Set, CollisionMath, RayCollisionTestClass


/// \fn void LineSegClass::Set(const LineSegClass &that, const Matrix3D &tm)
/// \brief Makes this segment a copy of another one carried through a
///        transform.
///
/// The way a ray is moved from world space into an object's own space before
/// it is cast against the object's geometry. MeshClass::Cast_Ray builds a
/// RayCollisionTestClass whose ray is the world ray transformed by the inverse
/// of the mesh's world matrix (first re-oriented toward the ray for models
/// flagged as aligned or oriented), which ends up here; the inline constructor
/// LineSegClass(const LineSegClass &, const Matrix3D &) also forwards to it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Both endpoints are transformed as points (Matrix3D::Transform_Vector,
/// rotation and translation). DP is recomputed as the difference of the new
/// endpoints. Dir is only rotated (Matrix3D::Rotate_Vector), and Length is
/// copied unchanged. The function does not renormalise or re-measure, so it
/// assumes tm is rigid.
///
/// \param that The segment to transform.
/// \param tm   The transform to apply. A scale or shear would leave Dir
///             not of unit length and Length inconsistent with DP.
///
/// \warning Only valid for a transform without scale or shear.
/// \see LineSegClass


/// \fn void LineSegClass::Set_Random(const Vector3 &min, const Vector3 &max)
/// \brief Gives the segment two random endpoints inside an axis-aligned
///        range, then recomputes its derived values.
///
/// Each of the six coordinates is drawn independently, as the range minimum
/// plus a random fraction of the range extent. DP, Dir and Length are then
/// computed the way the protected recalculate does. It had no named BFME 2
/// caller when this was written, and Zero Hour's game sources do not call it
/// either; it is presumably a test helper, like AABoxClass::Init_Random.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The fractions come from WWMath::Random_Float, which is the C runtime's rand
/// masked to 12 bits and divided by 4095, so there are 4,096 distinct values
/// from 0 to 1 inclusive. That is not the game's deterministic logic random
/// generator: it is the C runtime's own rand sequence, which other client-side
/// code also draws from (camera shake and light drawing call
/// WWMath::Random_Float) and reseeds (W3DVolumetricShadow::RenderMeshVolumeBounds
/// calls srand with a fixed constant, in both Zero Hour and BFME 2).
///
/// \param min Component-wise lower bound of both endpoints.
/// \param max Component-wise upper bound. It is not checked against min.
///
/// \warning Do not call this from game logic. It draws from the C runtime's
///          rand sequence, which render-side code also advances and reseeds
///          and which is not the logic random generator, so a logic caller's
///          results could differ between peers and break lockstep and replays.
/// \see AABoxClass::Init_Random


/// \fn Vector3 LineSegClass::Find_Point_Closest_To(const Vector3 &pos) const
/// \brief Returns the point of the segment nearest to a position, clamped to
///        the endpoints.
///
/// W3DRoadBuffer::findCrossTypeJoinVector calls it. Zero Hour: the same
/// function measures the distance from a road's end point to the centre line of
/// another road's straight segment, to decide whether the two roads should
/// join.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The offset from P0 to pos is dotted with the unit direction. If the result
/// is not positive the answer is P0, and if it reaches Length the answer is P1.
/// Otherwise it is P0 plus that distance along Dir. The perpendicular is
/// never needed.
///
/// \param pos The position to project, in the segment's space.
/// \return A copy of the nearest point of the segment. A zero-length segment
///         returns P0.
/// \see LineSegClass::Find_Intersection


/// \fn bool LineSegClass::Find_Intersection(const LineSegClass &other_line, Vector3 *p1, float *fraction1, Vector3 *p2, float *fraction2) const
/// \brief Finds the nearest points between the infinite lines that carry this
///        segment and another; reports false only if they are parallel.
///
/// Despite the name, this is a closest-points query between lines, not an
/// intersection test between segments. The results are not clamped to either
/// segment: p1 may lie beyond either end of this segment and p2 beyond either
/// end of other_line, and a fraction can be negative or greater than 1. If the
/// lines really cross, p1 and p2 coincide; the caller decides whether that is
/// close enough and whether the fractions are acceptable.
///
/// Three uses show why that design is useful. Zero Hour: W3DRoadBuffer extends a
/// join line until it meets the line of a road edge, which wants the infinite
/// lines, and takes the first point as the new corner; BFME 2's
/// W3DRoadBuffer::insertCrossTypeJoins, W3DRoadBuffer::miter and
/// W3DRoadBuffer::insertCurveSegmentAt call it. The ray casts of
/// SegmentedLineClass and StreakLineClass use the ray as this segment and each
/// line segment as the other, then treat it as a hit if the two points are
/// closer than the line's width and the ray fraction is in range (Zero Hour:
/// segline.cpp and streak.cpp show that logic; BFME 2's Cast_Ray methods call
/// this function). The third is BFME 2's, with no Zero Hour counterpart found:
/// the pathfinder's path-corner rounding in the GameLogic AI code offsets the
/// two legs of a turn sideways by the rounding radius and takes the first
/// returned point as the centre of the corner arc, around which it appends path
/// nodes. The full caller list comes from tools/callers_of.py.
///
/// \warning Not only render-side. Because the AI path-smoothing code appends
///          path nodes computed from this function's result, a change to its
///          arithmetic (including the parallel test, the division by Length
///          noted below, or float precision) can change path nodes and break
///          lockstep and replay compatibility.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// This is the closest-points formula for two lines with unit directions D1 and
/// D2. With c1 the cross product of D1 and D2, and w the cross of the offset
/// between the two start points with D2, the distance along this line is
/// (w dot c1) divided by (c1 dot c1); the same with the roles swapped gives
/// the distance along the other line. p1 is this line's start plus that
/// distance along its direction, p2 the same for the other line. The function
/// returns false when either denominator is exactly zero, which happens for
/// parallel lines and for a zero-length segment. Nearly parallel lines are not
/// detected and give large, imprecise distances.
///
/// \param other_line The second segment, whose line is used.
/// \param p1         [out] Nearest point on this segment's line.
/// \param fraction1  [out] Position of p1 along this segment, as the distance
///                   divided by Length. May be null.
/// \param p2         [out] Nearest point on the other segment's line.
/// \param fraction2  [out] Position of p2 along the other segment, may be null.
///                   Divided by this segment's Length and not the other's, so
///                   it is a true fraction of other_line only if both
///                   segments have the same length.
/// \return true if the lines are not parallel and the outputs were written;
///         false otherwise, in which case nothing is written.
///
/// \note The division of fraction2 by this segment's Length is in Zero Hour's
///       source and in the retail body. The Zero Hour callers seen either pass
///       null for fraction2 or pass a variable they never read.
/// \see LineSegClass::Find_Point_Closest_To, CollisionMath


/// \class TriClass
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_wwmath_line_tri_frustum
/// \brief A triangle as the collision code sees it: three vertex pointers and
///        one normal pointer, owning no data.
///
/// TriClass holds N, a pointer to the triangle's normal, and V[0] to V[2],
/// pointers to its vertices. It has no constructor, no destructor and no
/// storage of its own: the pointers refer to vertex and normal data owned by a
/// mesh, a terrain buffer or a local variable, and they must stay valid for as
/// long as the TriClass is used. The header says the class exists so that the
/// low-level collision code is independent of the render objects (it grew out
/// of Commando's terrain collision code).
///
/// The normal is a stored input: callers either point N at a plane normal they
/// already have or fill one with Compute_Normal, an inline in tri.h that
/// writes through N the normalised cross product of V[1] minus V[0] and V[2]
/// minus V[0], so counter-clockwise vertices give a normal facing the viewer.
/// BaseHeightMapRenderObjClass::Cast_Ray and W3DTerrainBackground::castTriangle
/// call it. Consumers read N directly: the sweep tests copy it into the result
/// normal, and Contains_Point uses only its largest component.
///
/// \par How BFME 2 uses it
/// CollisionMath::Collide for a line segment against a triangle finds where the
/// segment crosses the triangle's plane and then asks Contains_Point whether
/// that point is inside the triangle; terrain and mesh ray casts call it
/// (BaseHeightMapRenderObjClass::Cast_Ray, W3DTerrainBackground::castTriangle,
/// MeshGeometryClass::cast_ray_brute_force, AABTreeClass::Cast_Ray_To_Polys).
/// The plane and box tests in CollisionMath take triangles as TriClass.
/// tri.h also defines free inline helpers, among them Point_In_Triangle_2D and
/// Cast_Semi_Infinite_Axis_Aligned_Ray_To_Triangle, which this group does not
/// document.
///
/// \see TriClass::Contains_Point, CollisionMath


/// \fn bool TriClass::Contains_Point(const Vector3 &ipoint) const
/// \brief Tests whether a point on the triangle's plane lies inside the
///        triangle, by projecting everything to two axes.
///
/// The second half of a ray-triangle test. CollisionMath::Collide for a line
/// segment against a triangle intersects the segment with the triangle's
/// plane first, then calls this with the hit point. That was the only named
/// caller when this was written; Zero Hour has the same one.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The three coordinate axes are reduced to the two that remain after dropping
/// the one where the normal has its largest component (find_dominant_plane),
/// which projects the triangle onto the plane it faces most directly and keeps
/// the projection from collapsing. For each of the three edges the 2D cross
/// product of the edge and the vector from the edge's start to the point is
/// computed, and the sign is recorded as "not negative". The point is inside
/// when all three signs agree, so the test works for either winding.
///
/// A point exactly on an edge has a zero cross product, which counts as not
/// negative. Such a point is therefore accepted when the triangle's projected
/// interior has non-negative products and rejected when it has negative ones,
/// so edge hits depend on the triangle's winding in the projection.
///
/// The test works from the point's coordinates on the two kept axes and ignores
/// the third, so the point need not lie exactly on the plane. Nothing is
/// checked: N and all three vertex pointers must be valid. N only needs the
/// right largest component; it does not have to be normalised.
///
/// \param ipoint The point to test, usually the intersection of a segment with
///               the triangle's plane.
/// \return true if the projected point is inside the projected triangle, or
///         on an edge on the accepted side.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's file carries older point-in-triangle variants that are
///       compiled out; the cross-product version is the live one in Zero Hour
///       and its structure is what the BFME 2 body matches.
/// \see find_dominant_plane, TriClass::Find_Dominant_Plane


/// \fn void TriClass::Find_Dominant_Plane(int *axis1, int *axis2) const
/// \brief Reports which two coordinate axes span the plane the triangle faces
///        most directly, as indices 0 to 2.
///
/// The dominant axis is the one with the largest absolute component in the
/// triangle's normal; the two returned axes are the other two, lower index
/// first. They are (1, 2) when X dominates, (0, 2) when Y dominates and (0, 1)
/// when Z dominates. Ties go to the earlier axis, because only a strictly
/// larger component takes over. It had no named BFME 2 caller when this was
/// written; in Zero Hour only the compiled-out variants in
/// TriClass::Contains_Point call it, and Contains_Point uses the file-local
/// find_dominant_plane instead.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The three components of N are read as absolute values (the sign bit is
/// cleared), compared in X, Y, Z order, and the matching pair of indices is
/// written.
///
/// \param axis1 [out] Receives the lower index of the pair.
/// \param axis2 [out] Receives the higher index of the pair.
///
/// \see find_dominant_plane, TriClass::Contains_Point


/// \fn static void find_dominant_plane(const TriClass &tri, int *axis1, int *axis2, int *axis3)
/// \brief File-local form of TriClass::Find_Dominant_Plane that also reports
///        the dominant axis itself.
///
/// TriClass::Contains_Point calls it. The selection is the same as in
/// TriClass::Find_Dominant_Plane: the axis with the largest absolute normal
/// component, ties to the earlier axis. The two other axes come back in axis1
/// and axis2 and the dominant axis in axis3.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// X dominant gives (1, 2, 0), Y gives (0, 2, 1) and Z gives (0, 1, 2) for
/// (axis1, axis2, axis3). Retail contains this as a separate function that
/// Contains_Point calls rather than inlining it. The name is the Zero Hour
/// source's; it is not an export, so the retail name is not known.
///
/// \param tri   The triangle whose normal selects the axes. Its N must be
///              valid.
/// \param axis1 [out] Lower index of the two axes perpendicular to the
///              dominant one.
/// \param axis2 [out] Higher index of that pair.
/// \param axis3 [out] The dominant axis.
///
/// \see TriClass::Find_Dominant_Plane, TriClass::Contains_Point
